From ee26f78f014b63291ca40be8cdc048e849168986 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: albanread <albanread@googlemail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 18:08:55 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Sound: virtio_sound hmulti_audio driver, media stack in the
 image
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

drivers/audio/virtio/virtio_sound: a real hmulti_audio driver for the
virtio sound device (virtio 1.2 §5.14) in place of the stub. One stereo
16-bit output stream at 48 kHz (44.1 kHz fallback): PCM_INFO picks the
stream, SET_PARAMS/PREPARE/START/STOP/RELEASE over the controlq, the
Multi Audio buffers the mixer writes into are the txq buffers themselves
(2-4 buffers of up to 4096 frames, physically contiguous), and a txq
completion is the "buffer played" event the exchange waits for.

The exchange hands buffers out on a steady schedule derived from the
sample rate, anchored to the device's completions and re-anchored only
if the device falls a whole period behind: hosts consume in bursts of
their own audio period (QEMU: 10 ms timer) and the multi_audio node
drops a mixer buffer that arrives while the previous one is unconsumed,
so a bursty cadence produced a silent buffer every few periods. Buffers
stay mapped across BUFFER_FORCE_STOP (the node still writes silence into
them afterwards; freeing them crashed media_addon_server).

device_manager: publish_directories() now enumerates the installed driver
directories after boot too (upstream left that as a TODO). Without it
/dev/audio never existed on a system without a legacy audio driver, so a
device-manager audio driver was never probed.

virtio_pci: interrupt handlers return B_INVOKE_SCHEDULER after queue
callbacks, which wake driver threads.

Image: libgame/libmedia/libmidi2, media_server, media_addon_server,
midi_server, the mixer, hmulti_audio and tone_producer_demo add-ons,
the virtio_sound driver, and prose_tone (BSoundPlayer sine generator)
for testing without a media player.

Verified: QEMU's wav backend records a clean 440 Hz tone (0 glitches,
0 re-anchors); VZ's virtio-snd plays through the Mac (195 buffers, 0
errors, device lag 0 us).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
